(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_plantkingdom=[{id:"neet-2026-q100",pn:100,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Gymnosperms",q:"In which one of the following, the ovules are not enclosed by an ovary wall and remain exposed?",o:["Selaginella","Funaria","Pinus","Wolffia"],c:2,s:`Gymnosperms are plants whose ovules are not enclosed by an ovary wall and stay exposed, both before and after fertilisation.
Pinus is a gymnosperm.
Funaria is a moss.
Selaginella is a pteridophyte.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q102",pn:102,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Gymnosperms",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: In gymnosperms, the male and female gametophytes remain within the sporangia.
Statement II: In gymnosperms, seeds are not covered.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:3,s:`In gymnosperms the male and female gametophytes have no independent free-living existence.
They stay within the sporangia, retained on the sporophyte.
Gymnosperm ovules are not enclosed by any ovary wall and stay exposed before and after fertilisation.
The seeds that develop after fertilisation are not covered, i.e. naked.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q130",pn:130,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Pteridophytes",q:"Sphenopsida class belongs to ______________.",o:["angiosperms","gymnosperms","pteridophytes","bryophytes"],c:2,s:"Class Sphenopsida belongs to pteridophytes."},{id:"neet-2026r-q131",pn:131,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Life cycles of plants",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Fusion of protoplasms between gametes \u2014 I. Meiosis
B. Fusion of two nuclei \u2014 II. Plasmogamy
C. Generation of haploid spores \u2014 III. Karyogamy
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-II, B-I, C-III","A-III, B-II, C-I","A-I, B-III, C-II","A-II, B-III, C-I"],c:3,s:`Plasmogamy is fusion of protoplasms (A-II).
Karyogamy is fusion of two nuclei (B-III).
Meiosis leads to the production of haploid spores (C-I).`},{id:"neet-2025-q111",pn:111,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Pteridophyte life cycle",q:`Given below are the stages in the life cycle of pteridophytes. Arrange the following stages in the correct sequence.
A. Prothallus stage
B. Meiosis in spore mother cells
C. Fertilisation
D. Formation of archegonia and antheridia in gametophyte.
E. Transfer of antherozoids to the archegonia in presence of water.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["B, A, D, E, C","B, A, E, C, D","D, E, C, A, B","E, D, C, B, A"],c:0,s:`The correct sequence in a pteridophyte life cycle is:
B \u2192 Meiosis in spore mother cells
A \u2192 Prothallus stage
D \u2192 Formation of archegonia and antheridia in gametophyte
E \u2192 Transfer of antherozoids to the archegonia in presence of water
C \u2192 Fertilisation
So, the correct sequence is B \u2192 A \u2192 D \u2192 E \u2192 C`},{id:"neet-2025-q119",pn:119,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Bryophyte life cycle",q:`The correct sequence of events in the life cycle of bryophytes is
A. Fusion of antherozoid with egg.
B. Attachment of gametophyte to substratum.
C. Reduction division to produce haploid spores.
D. Formation of sporophyte.
E. Release of antherozoids into water.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["D, E, A, C, B","B, E, A, C, D","B, E, A, D, C","D, E, A, B, C"],c:2,s:`The correct sequence of events in the life cycle of bryophytes is:
Attachment of gametophyte to substratum.
Release of antherozoids into water.
Fusion of antherozoid with egg.
Formation of sporophyte.
Reduction division to produce haploid spores.`},{id:"neet-2025-q159",pn:159,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Gymnosperms",q:"Which one of the following is the characteristic feature of gymnosperms?",o:["Seeds are enclosed in fruits","Seeds are naked","Seeds are absent","Gymnosperms have flowers for reproduction"],c:1,s:`Gymnosperms (Gymnos: naked, sperma: seed) are plants whose ovules are not enclosed by an ovary wall.
The ovules stay exposed before and after fertilization.
The seeds that develop after fertilization are not covered, i.e., naked.`},{id:"neet-2025-q162",pn:162,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Plant groups examples",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I \u2014 List-II
A. Pteridophyte \u2014 I. Salvia
B. Bryophyte \u2014 II. Ginkgo
C. Angiosperm \u2014 III. Polytrichum
D. Gymnosperm \u2014 IV. Salvinia
Choose the option with all correct matches.`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],c:1,s:`Pteridophyte - Salvinia.
Bryophyte - Polytrichum.
Angiosperm - Salvia.
Gymnosperm - Ginkgo.`},{id:"neet-2025-q168",pn:168,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Bryophytes",q:"In bryophytes, the gemmae help in which one of the following?",o:["Sexual reproduction","Asexual reproduction","Nutrient absorption","Gaseous exchange"],c:1,s:`Gemmae are green, multicellular, asexual buds.
They develop in small receptacles called gemma cups and help in asexual reproduction of bryophytes.`},{id:"neet-2024-q150",pn:150,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Phaeophyceae",q:`Read the following statements and choose the set of correct statements.
In the members of Phaeophyceae.
A. Asexual reproduction occurs usually by biflagellate zoospores.
B. Sexual reproduction is by oogamous method only.
C. Stored food is in the form of carbohydrates which is either mannitol or laminarin
D. The major pigments found are chlorophyll a, c and carotenoids and xanthophyll.
E. Vegetative cells have a cellulosic wall, usually covered on the outside by gelatinous coating of algin.
Choose the correct answer front the options given below:`,o:["A, C, D and E only","A, B, C and E only","A, B, C and D only","B, C, D and E only"],c:0,s:`In Phaeophyceae, sexual reproduction can be oogamous, isogamous or anisogamous.
So B is wrong, because it says oogamous only.
A, C, D and E are correct.`},{id:"neet-2023-q103",pn:103,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Pteridophytes: heterospory",q:"Identify the pair of heterosporous pteridophytes among the following :",o:["Equisetum and Salvinia","Lycopodium and Selaginella","Selaginella and Salvinia","Psilotum and Salvinia"],c:2,s:`Selaginella and Salvinia are heterosporous. They make two kinds of spores.
Psilotum, Lycopodium and Equisetum are homosporous.`},{id:"neet-2023-q114",pn:114,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Bryophytes: moss life cycle",q:`Given below are two statements : One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R :
Assertion A : The first stage of gametophyte in the life cycle of moss is protonema stage.
Reason R : Protonema develops directly from spores produced in capsule.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct"],c:1,s:`The main stage of the moss life cycle is the gametophyte, which has two stages.
The first stage is the protonema, which grows directly from a spore.
The capsule of the sporophyte holds the spores that give the protonema.
So R correctly explains A.`},{id:"neet-2023-q145",pn:145,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Gymnosperm pollination",q:`Given below are two statements : One labelled as Assertion A and the other labelled as Reason R :
Assertion A : In gymnosperms the pollen grains are released from the microsporangium and carried by air currents.
Reason R : Air currents carry the pollen grains to the mouth of the archegonia where the male gametes are discharged and pollen tube is not formed.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the current explanation of A","A is true but R is false"],c:3,s:`A is true: pollen is released from the microsporangium and carried by air.
Pollen reaches the opening of the ovules on megasporophylls.
R is false: a pollen tube does form and carries the male gametes towards the archegonia, discharging them near their mouth.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q112",pn:112,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Plant groups examples",q:`Match List-I with List-II :
List-I: (a) Chlamydomonas (b) Cycas (c) Selaginella (d) Sphagnum
List-II: (i) Moss (ii) Pteridophyte (iii) Alga (iv) Gymnosperm
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(a) - (ii), (b) - (iii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iv)","(a) - (iii), (b) - (i), (c) - (ii), (d) - (iv)","(a) - (iii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)","(a) - (iii), (b) - (ii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iv)"],c:2,s:`Chlamydomonas is a unicellular alga.
Cycas is a gymnosperm.
Selaginella is a pteridophyte.
Sphagnum is a moss.`},{id:"neet-2022-q115",pn:115,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Algae and their products",q:"Hydrocolloid carrageen is obtained from:",o:["Phaeophyceae and Rhodophyceae","Rhodophyceae only","Phaeophyceae only","Chlorophyceae and Phaeophyceae"],c:1,s:`Hydrocolloids hold water.
Carrageen comes from red algae (Rhodophyceae).`},{id:"neet-2022-q121",pn:121,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Algae reserve food",q:"Which of the following is incorrectly matched?",o:["Ulothrix \u2013 Mannitol","Porphyra \u2013 Floridian Starch","Volvox \u2013 Starch","Ectocarpus \u2013 Fucoxanthin"],c:0,s:`Ulothrix is a green alga and stores starch.
Mannitol is the stored food of brown algae.`},{id:"neet-2022-q142",pn:142,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Life cycle patterns",q:`Match the plant with the kind of life cycle it exhibits:
List-I: (a) Spirogyra (b) Fern (c) Funaria (d) Cycas
List-II: (i) Dominant diploid sporophyte vascular plant, with highly reduced male or female gametophyte (ii) Dominant haploid free-living gametophyte (iii) Dominant diploid sporophyte alternating with reduced gametophyte called prothallus (iv) Dominant haploid leafy gametophyte alternating with partially dependent multicellular sporophyte
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],c:0,s:`Spirogyra is an alga with a haplontic life cycle (dominant haploid gametophyte): (a)-(ii).
Fern is a pteridophyte; the sporophyte dominates and the gametophyte is the prothallus: (b)-(iii).
Funaria is a bryophyte; the leafy gametophyte dominates: (c)-(iv).
Cycas is a gymnosperm; the sporophyte dominates and the gametophyte is highly reduced: (d)-(i).`},{id:"neet-2022r-q142",pn:142,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Chara features",q:`Read the following statements and identify the characters related to the alga shown in the diagram
(a) It is a member of Chlorophyceae
(b) Food is stored in the from of starch
(c) It is a monoecious plant showing oogonium and antheridium
(d) Food is stored in the form of laminarin or mannitol
(e) It shows dominance of pigments Chlorophyll a, c and Fucoxanthin
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(c), (d) and (e) only","(a) and (b) only","(a), (b) and (c) only","(a), (c) and (d) only"],c:2,s:`The alga is Chara, a green alga (Chlorophyceae).
Its food is stored as starch around a protein core in pyrenoids.
It is monoecious, with male and female organs on the same plant.
Laminarin, mannitol, and chlorophyll a, c with fucoxanthin belong to brown algae, so (d) and (e) are wrong.`,fig:{w:165,h:250,alt:"Chara plant: a slender upright axis with nodes, each node bearing a whorl of slender branchlets, whorls smaller towards the tip.",d:[["p","M62 236C66 226 74 205 80 188C86 172 87 150 85 129C83 110 80 92 77 75C75 60 73 45 72 28","B"],["p","M77 75Q63.9 61.3 55.3 68"],["p","M77 75Q64.7 60.1 57.5 59.7"],["p","M77 75Q67.2 58.9 62.8 52.3"],["p","M77 75Q71.1 57.7 71 46.8"],["p","M77 75Q82.9 57.7 83 46.8"],["p","M77 75Q86.8 58.9 91.2 52.3"],["p","M77 75Q89.3 60.1 96.5 59.7"],["p","M77 75Q90.1 61.3 98.7 68"],["p","M85 129Q64.9 108 51.8 118.2"],["p","M85 129Q66.2 106.2 55 105.6"],["p","M85 129Q70 104.3 63.2 94.2"],["p","M85 129Q76 102.5 75.8 85.8"],["p","M85 129Q94 102.5 94.2 85.8"],["p","M85 129Q100 104.3 106.8 94.2"],["p","M85 129Q103.8 106.2 115 105.6"],["p","M85 129Q105.1 108 118.2 118.2"],["p","M80 188Q61.6 168.8 49.6 178.1"],["p","M80 188Q62.8 167.2 52.6 166.6"],["p","M80 188Q66.3 165.5 60.1 156.2"],["p","M80 188Q71.8 163.8 71.6 148.6"],["p","M80 188Q88.2 163.8 88.4 148.6"],["p","M80 188Q93.7 165.5 99.9 156.2"],["p","M80 188Q97.2 167.2 107.4 166.6"],["p","M80 188Q98.4 168.8 110.4 178.1"],["p","M70 220Q53.4 202.7 42.5 211.1"],["p","M70 220Q54.5 201.2 45.2 200.7"],["p","M70 220Q57.6 199.6 52 191.2"],["p","M70 220Q62.6 198.1 62.4 184.3"],["p","M70 220Q77.4 198.1 77.6 184.3"],["p","M70 220Q82.4 199.6 88 191.2"],["p","M70 220Q85.5 201.2 94.8 200.7"],["p","M70 220Q86.6 202.7 97.5 211.1"],["p","M72 28Q65.8 18.4 62.8 14.9"],["p","M72 28Q67.9 18.4 67.1 12.8"],["p","M72 28Q72 18.4 72 12"],["p","M72 28Q76.1 18.4 76.9 12.8"],["p","M72 28Q78.2 18.4 81.2 14.9"]]}},{id:"neet-2021-q102",pn:102,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Sexuality in plants",q:"Which of the following plants is monoecious?",o:["Cycas circinalis","Carica papaya","Chara","Marchantia polymorpha"],c:2,s:`A monoecious plant has male and female sex organs on the same plant.
Most Chara species are monoecious.
Cycas, papaya and Marchantia have male and female organs on different plants (dioecious).`},{id:"neet-2021-q120",pn:120,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Asexual reproduction in bryophytes",q:"Gemmae are present in",o:["Some Liverworts","Mosses","Pteridophytes","Some Gymnosperms"],c:0,s:`Gemmae are green multicellular buds made in gemma cups of some liverworts such as Marchantia, used for asexual reproduction.
Mosses multiply by fragmentation and budding of protonema.`},{id:"neet-2021-q125",pn:125,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Algae",q:"Which of the following algae produce Carrageen?",o:["Blue-green algae","Green algae","Brown algae","Red algae"],c:3,s:`The red algae (Rhodophyceae) give agar and carrageen from their cell walls.
Brown algae give algin.`},{id:"neet-2021-q132",pn:132,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Algae reserve food",q:"Which of the following algae contains mannitol as reserve food material?",o:["Ulothrix","Ectocarpus","Gracilaria","Volvox"],c:1,s:`Ectocarpus is a brown alga; brown algae store mannitol and laminarin.
Ulothrix and Volvox (green) store starch; Gracilaria (red) stores floridean starch.`},{id:"neet-2021-q133",pn:133,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Heterospory",q:"Genera like Selaginella and Salvinia produce two kinds of spores. Such plants are known as:",o:["Heterosporous","Homosorus","Heterosorus","Homosporous"],c:0,s:`Selaginella and Salvinia make small microspores and large megaspores, two kinds of spores.
Plants like this are heterosporous; homosporous plants make one kind.`}]})();
